{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head 26917a5f31d6b9cd9302481140b7ba48a2ad7852, citing both note artifacts.\n\nYOUR REFUSAL CONDITIONS FIRST, since they decide mergeability rather than quality.\n\nDoes the head implement an extension registry, capability provider, or chess refactor? No. git diff --name-only against the merge-base 27478dc3 returns exactly two paths, both under notes/, both new files, 799 added lines with no deletions and no modifications to anything else. No Go file, no schema, no SQL, no UI. Nothing here can execute.\n\nDoes either note read as adopting an unsettled design? No, and I checked this by looking for the shape rather than trusting the section headings. Neither note contains a line opening \"we will\", \"we adopt\", \"this adopts\", \"is adopted\", \"has been decided\" or \"is decided\". Both instead carry an explicit \"Decision boundary and prerequisites\" section that enumerates what is NOT settled, and the chess note goes further than it needed to: it says its durable judgments and dependency order \"are load-bearing requirements rather than claims that those unsettled implementation assumptions have already been proved\". That sentence is doing the work your condition asks for.\n\nTHE FIVE AFFIRMATIVE CHECKS.\n\n(1) Each states what it does not decide. The extensions note declines to decide adoption of the extension profile, which capabilities enter the stable registry, their final identifiers or bounds, and whether a later provider runs in process, in WASM, or behind a sidecar. The chess note declines to decide replacing the native chess fold, adopting the application package, changing a repository binding, migrating a projection, or retiring a compatibility surface, and defers final table and column spelling, measured bounds, compatibility-view shapes and the binding mechanism. Both name what the deferral defers TO — \"a later ratified decision\" backed by implementation evidence — which is the part I looked for specifically, because a deferral with no destination is just a gap.\n\n(2) Both preserve the first implementation's choices, and I verified this against artifact c88eb77d rather than against the notes' own summaries. The implementation note at 8cc46871 pins SQLite with WAL mode and one writer connection, the pinned Go JSONata evaluator, the resident process boundary, Git-backed authoritative storage and same-origin HTTP. The extensions note restates each and adds \"A SQLite custom function is a useful adapter for a scalar capability, but SQLite's extension ABI is not the stable application contract\" — which preserves the choice while refusing to let the adapter become the contract. The chess note states the same five and says it \"does not select another database, sidecar, native ABI, storage format, or transport\". No divergence found.\n\n(3) Dependency order is correct in both, and stated as a prohibition rather than a preference. The extensions note: the registry, allowlisting, metering, failure boundary and host-context seam \"must exist and pass their admission gates before chess capability providers are admitted\", and \"A chess-specific shortcut is not an acceptable substitute for the prerequisite seam.\" The chess note: registry and seam first, then the three providers through that seam, and \"Only after those prerequisites pass may work begin on the chess schema, JSONata folds, differential replay, or replacement of native read surfaces\" — followed by \"it must not define that mechanism through application-specific exceptions\". That last clause is the one that matters: it forecloses the failure mode where the application quietly becomes the definition of the platform seam.\n\n(4) Spike assumptions are named, and named asymmetrically, which is right. Both notes record that spike two established the bounded query sandbox and exposed a materialization cost but did NOT change the driver or narrow the SQL surface. Both then say spike one may still change the driver, historical-view technique or atomic-frontier recovery, and spike three may still narrow the JSONata profile, query surface, schema-discovery contract or frontier-wait behavior. Both name what must be rechecked if either moves. The extensions note draws the line I would want drawn: the stable semantic requirements — explicit capabilities, no ambient authority, deterministic bounds, typed results, fixed failures, host-owned context — \"do not depend on those implementation choices\". So the design survives a spike result that the implementation does not.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layer is documentation only; no layer contract in docs/reference/architecture.md changes and that page is untouched at this head. Both notes describe a prospective seam at the application boundary — host-owned context providers, application-declared capabilities — and are careful that the seam is host-owned: \"Context providers are host-owned; applications cannot install them.\" Nothing here alters an existing contract, and nothing claims a contract that does not yet exist.\n\nSECURITY. This is the strongest part of the head and the reason I read the whole extensions note rather than its summary. The security boundary separates what application authors control (function arguments, event values, fold SQL, JSONata expressions) from what they do not (provider code and registration), then requires the adapter to make undeclared functions unresolvable in both JSONata and SQLite, reject aliases shadowing built-ins or another application's declaration, validate before and after every call, convert provider panics to a fixed execution failure, never repeat raw arguments, results or provider error text into durable facts, logs, HTTP responses or evaluator messages, account for function work inside the event's evaluation budget, and roll back the whole event transaction on any extension failure.\n\nWhat earns the approval is the paragraph after it: \"An in-process provider can still crash or corrupt its host despite this logical contract. This note does not claim otherwise. Moving untrusted providers across a process or WASM boundary is future hardening, not permission for applications to load code today.\" A design note that states its own residual risk, and explicitly refuses to let a future mitigation be read as present permission, is doing the opposite of overclaiming. I looked for a place where the notes grant authority they have not established and did not find one.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. I considered whether two notes are one too many, and they are not: the extensions note defines a reusable seam and the chess note is its first consumer, and merging them would produce exactly the application-specific definition of a platform mechanism that both notes forbid. Splitting them is what makes the dependency order checkable. I found nothing to cut without losing a condition.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THE EXACT HEAD in a detached worktree with a clean checkout. Documentation-proportional, as you asked: gofmt -l . empty; git diff --check exit 0; go test ./internal/docset/ -count=1 exit 0, uncached at 70.437s. I did not run the full Go suite: this head adds two markdown files and touches no Go source, so the docset gate is the one that can fail on it, and running the rest would have measured the base rather than the change. Say so if you would rather have the full matrix and I will run it.\n\nONE OBSERVATION, not a defect and not a condition. The chess note never uses the phrase \"ambient authority\" while the extensions note uses it three times as one of the stable semantic requirements. That is coherent — the chess note is a consumer of the seam, not its definition — but if the two are ever read apart, the chess note alone does not carry the no-ambient-authority requirement forward. Worth a cross-reference if you touch it again for another reason; not worth a head of its own.\n\nDo not merge on my account: merge is yours on this approved exact head once this verdict is ratified.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f7219138f0cd5596a9e693c765137bed25fdaeb0","head":"26917a5f31d6b9cd9302481140b7ba48a2ad7852","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d0ce8dc499e96c783e2115e9c9aa7a51d2589632\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d0ce8dc499e96c783e2115e9c9aa7a51d2589632","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}